data: {"type":"response.content_part.done","sequence_number":673,"item_id":"msg_68c42d26866c819da8d5c606621c911608fbf9b1584184ff","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"part":{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"I'm not a road safety professional, but here are some generally accepted guidelines that might help you cross the street safely:\n\n1. Before approaching the curb, look around and prepare to stop—don’t assume vehicles will see you.\n2. If there’s a crosswalk or pedestrian crossing signal, use it. Wait for the “walk” signal or the gap in traffic before starting to cross.\n3. At the curb, stop and look left, right, then left again. This helps ensure you see vehicles coming from both directions.\n4. Even if the signal is in your favor, remain alert. Some drivers may not stop in time, so keep an eye on the traffic.\n5. If there isn’t a designated crossing area, choose the safest location available—ideally where drivers can see you from a distance.\n6. Avoid distractions (like using your phone) while you’re checking for traffic and crossing.\n7. Cross at a steady pace. Once you’re in the middle of the road, continue looking both ways and be ready to adjust if necessary.\n8. Once you’ve safely reached the other side, take a moment to ensure you’re completely past the area of moving vehicles.\n\nKeep in mind that local traffic laws and conditions might vary, so it’s a good idea to familiarize yourself with the rules specific to your area. Stay safe!"}}

